F = {
"species|Reborn": "Reborn have died and yet live on, and nobody can say why. Some wear death's marks, such as ashen skin or bloodless veins, while others are built by magic or science from many parts. All of them chase answers and a life of their own.",
"species|Rimekin": "Rimekin are flamekin who answered the Phyrexian invasion with cold reasoning instead of rash reaction. Their innate flames turned icy blue and give off chill instead of heat, and armor and weapons in their hands gather spiky, harmless frost. They live in both Lorwyn and Shadowmoor.",
"species|Satyr": "Satyrs are cheerful, social folk who love a good revel and treat life as a gift to enjoy. They look humanlike, but goat horns, pointed ears, hooves, and furred legs set them apart. They tease the solemn builders and philosophers of Theros and want to taste everything the world offers.",
"species|Shifter": "Shifters descend from people touched by lycanthropy. They cannot take a full beast form, but they can briefly sharpen their animal traits, which they call shifting. They are lithe and human in size, with big eyes, pointed ears, long canines, and fur-like hair, yet always clearly Humanoid.",
"species|Simic Hybrid": "Simic hybrids are humans, elves, or vedalken reshaped by Simic Combine magic with animal traits, mostly from sea, reptile, or amphibian creatures. Claws, tentacles, fins, and toothy maws are common. The Combine made them as adapted soldiers for its Guardian Project and treats any who leave as deserters.",
"species|Siren": "Sirens are birdlike humanoids of the Stormwreck Sea, living on rocky coasts, remote islands, and floating kelp. Their moods swing fast from warm to cruel and back. They love ships and toy with them, though a growing number now join crews to feed their curiosity, one even as captain.",
"species|Tabaxi": "The Cat Lord, a divine being of the Upper Planes, made tabaxi to blend humanoids with cats. Some tabaxi live like true cats, curious and playful, while others live like any other folk. Their fur might show big cat stripes, a house cat's coat, or a pattern all their own.",
"species|Thri-kreen": "Thri-kreen are insect folk with four arms and a chitin shell that shifts color to match their surroundings. They never sleep, but rest in still periods while staying fully alert. They speak through clacking mandibles and waving antennae, which others cannot read, so they use telepathy with outsiders.",
"species|Tiefling": "Tieflings are born in the Lower Planes or descend from Fiends, and the link is in their blood. This legacy brings power but does not set their morals, and each tiefling decides whether to embrace or regret it. Three legacies exist: abyssal, chthonic, and infernal, each with its own looks.",
"species|Tortle": "Tortles are turtle folk who carry their homes on their backs and roam coasts, rivers, and seas on many worlds. They tell no single tale of their origin, but all feel a mystic bond with nature. Their shell gives them security anywhere, and many decorate it with care.",
"species|Triton": "Tritons are amphibious folk of the Siren Sea, living among coral forests and hidden undersea mountains. Scaled, crested, and finned, they swim as fast as they run and craft gear from coral, shell, and bone. Most stay below, but curious ones climb out to explore the dry world.",
"species|Uncommon Races": "Dragonborn, gnomes, half-elves, and half-orcs are less common than dwarves, elves, halflings, and humans. Big cities barely notice them, but small villages react more. Dragonborn draw wary caution, gnomes win people over with humor, half-elves attract gossip, and half-orcs are often assumed to be short-tempered.",
"species|Vampire": "Zendikar's vampires were bred into servitude and now live on the energy in blood, strongest in fear and pain. They are not undead but carry a disease that chills their gray or purple skin. A civil war splits them between free traditionalists and those bound again to the Eldrazi.",
"species|Vampire (Ixalan)": "The Legion of Dusk is ruled by vampires who are faster, stronger, and gifted with supernatural power. Some see the Rite of Redemption as sacrifice for a glorious age and carry an imperial pride. Others use vampirism to gain an edge, and they are cruel and ambitious conquerors seeking the Immortal Sun.",
"species|Vedalken": "Vedalken believe nothing is perfect and enjoy that, since every flaw invites improvement. They are tall, slender, hairless, and blue skinned, with broad noses and no outer ears, and they talk about ideas but keep their lives private. Others find them cold, though they feel deeply and simply hide it well.",
"species|Verdan": "The verdan are goblins and hobgoblins changed when they passed through the shadow of That-Which-Endures, a mysterious entity of chaos. They now have jade skin, black blood, pointed ears, and limited telepathy, but lost their history. They fled to the surface and are the newest people in Faerûn.",
"species|Warforged": "Warforged are sentient constructs built as weapons for the Last War, yet they feel pain and emotion. Cords soaked in alchemical fluid act as muscle around a frame of steel, darkwood, or stone, with armored plates over it. Those who grow into individuals often seek an artificer to reshape their bodies.",
"species|Yuan-Ti": "Yuan-ti were once humans who remade themselves into serpent folk through ancient rites. Most of those rites twisted them into monsters, but some produced a people that mixes human and snake traits. They resist magic and poison and show it through forked tongues, snake eyes, or similar features.",
"bg|Aberrant Heir": "Your aberrant dragonmark has made life hard. You may have hidden it well, or met fear and hostility from dragonmarked houses. You might have joined House Tarkanan's criminals, or you might walk your own road, trusting your wits and your mark to carry you.",
"bg|Acolyte": "You spent your life serving a temple to a god or pantheon, performing rites and offerings that bring worshipers close to the divine. This is not the same as being a cleric. You may have been a child helper, a sudden high priest, or the head of a small cult.",
"bg|Agent of the Ninth Quill": "Your sharp mind and thieving skill led you to the Ninth Quill, a network of thieves, saboteurs, and spies who take magical lore and relics from others. Stealing items and tomes and sabotaging mages taught you cunning and a few magical tricks for spy work.",
"bg|Anthropologist": "You are fascinated by cultures, from ancient lost lands to modern cities. You studied customs, laws, rituals, beliefs, languages, and art to learn how societies shape their own fates. Books and scrolls taught you part of it, and visits to distant settlements taught the rest.",
"bg|Archaeologist": "You spent your life studying fallen cultures by visiting ruins, reading their records, and examining what they left behind. You may have trained at Morgrave University and worked in remote digs. Some archaeologists loot for fame or coin, but most feel called to learn, mixing scholarship with treasure hunting grit.",
"bg|Artisan": "As soon as you could carry a bucket, you cleaned floors in an artisan's workshop for a few coppers a day. Later you apprenticed, learned to make simple goods, and learned to charm picky customers. Your trade also gave you a sharp eye for detail.",
"bg|Astral Drifter": "You have wandered the Astral Sea longer than you can recall. There you stopped aging, hunger, and thirst, and you drifted between its regions out of wanderlust. You have camped on the husks of dead gods, dodged psychic winds, and avoided the plane's deadliest inhabitants.",
"bg|Athlete": "You push yourself to perfect body and technique, and you feel alive in competition with a cheering crowd. Stories of your feats open doors and loosen tongues. In Theros, contests and those who pursue them earn near reverent respect, and athletes of every origin often meet each other.",
"bg|Augen Trust (Spy)": "The Augen Trust is a secret network of imperial spies in the Dwendalian Empire, led by the gnome Oliver Schreiber, who alone knows every member. Betraying it is high treason, and defectors are hunted. If you are an adventurer, you either hid your identity or remain on duty.",
"bg|Azorius Functionary": "You proudly serve the Azorius Senate, the guild that writes and enforces the laws of Ravnica. With brutes at the gate and chaos looming, you believe order is all that prevents disaster. You help keep the city running safely and smoothly.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Acolyte": "Baldur's Gate holds believers of nearly every faith, with newcomers constantly arriving from distant lands. Most faiths are tolerated, though evil ones are pushed into the Twin Songs district. An acolyte from here might seek greater things, not for personal glory but for the sake of their faith.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Charlatan": "In Baldur's Gate everyone tries to get ahead of someone else. One group's swindler is another's revolutionary, or perhaps you simply look out for yourself. Either way, you have a plan to hit it big, and all you need is nerve and a bit of time.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Criminal": "Any career criminal in Baldur's Gate knows the Guild. Some keep quiet, slipping smuggled goods in with honest cargo, and others join crews or the Guild for protection. A few were thrown out for incompetence or for crossing someone powerful, and they now scrape by on scraps.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Entertainer": "Baldur's Gate offers a colorful range of performers, from the Oasis Theater's singers and acrobats to the Wide's mimes and shadow puppeteers. Good acts always find audiences, and the steady stream of travelers brings both fresh spectators and fresh spectacles through the city.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Folk Hero": "Baldur's Gate badly needs heroes, and now and then one rises from its own people. Locals love and remember such champions, but public opinion can turn on them nearly as fast as it first praised them.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Guild Artisan": "Baldur's Gate has many guilds and professional groups, covering every trade you can name, from gravediggers to moneylenders. As an artisan here, you belong to one of these organized crafts.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Hermit": "A hermit in a busy city may seem odd, but deep solitude can exist in the middle of a crowd. A small number of people in Baldur's Gate manage to find isolation amid all its noise and bustle.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Noble": "The patriars of Baldur's Gate live in the Upper City, hosting galas and keeping fashion, walled off from the poorer districts. They know the courts of far lands well but little of the poor at their door. Their feature replaces Position and Privilege, though a merchant noble may keep the standard one.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Outlander": "People come to Baldur's Gate for profit, excitement, and cosmopolitan chances, but few grasp the tangled social mess waiting there. You arrive as an outsider, and no matter how long you stay, you will likely remain one, if you ever leave at all.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Sage": "Baldur's Gate has a modest scholarly community around the High Hall libraries and temples of learning. Lecturers, researchers, and historians trade ideas with visiting scholars from Candlekeep. Arcane study also thrives, though wizards work alone in their own homes rather than in a shared community.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Sailor": "Sailors founded Baldur's Gate, and its harbor remains its heart. Several patriars descend from old captains, the Lower City's trade rests on the port, and river traffic sets the Outer City's pace. Sailors are as common here as the cobblestones, so this background is widespread.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Soldier": "Many kinds of soldiers walk the streets of Baldur's Gate, including mercenaries, private guards, Watch soldiers, and members of the Flaming Fist. You are one of them, and you earn your keep with arms and discipline.",
"bg|Baldur's Gate Urchin": "Orphans and runaways gather in the Outer City, chasing passersby in ragged crowds to beg for scraps. In the Lower City, urchins are often recruited into the Guild's lowest ranks or forced into dangerous work by ruthless masters.",
"bg|Bejeweled Conclave Spy": "In public you pose as a courtier, mediator, or entertainer, but your real talent is gathering secrets. The enchanters of the Bejeweled Conclave trained you to use charm, wit, and a little magic to infiltrate groups, uncover information, and broker deals.",
"bg|Boros Legionnaire": "You serve the Boros Legion, devoted to angels and to bringing justice and peace to Ravnica's streets. You might be a true believer moved by the angels and the downtrodden. You might instead be a cynic who followed a parent, fell for a recruiter's promises, or wanted an active life.",
"bg|Carouser": "You grew up in the heart of a big city like Baldur's Gate, spending countless evenings in taverns, theaters, parlors, and gambling dens. You read people well and learn their secrets over cards or at fine parties. You have been thrown out of a few places, but only by dull hosts.",
"bg|Celebrity Adventurer's Scion": "Your family name inspires fear and admiration, though you earned none of it. Songs celebrate your famous parent, who was seldom home and always talking of dragons and demons. The family fortune went to potions and ale, and everyone expects you to fight like your forebear, so proving yourself is hard.",
"bg|Charlatan": "You have always had a way with people. You sense what they want, draw out their desires in minutes, and promise to deliver it. Your pink tonics, silvery ointments, and bridges for sale sound too good to be true, yet you make them sound real.",
"bg|Chondathan Freebooter": "Chondath expects youths to serve four years in the military, but you resented that control. You gave up your nation and your name, then joined the first ship that would take you. You have roamed the Vilhon Reach ever since, with deep local ties and broad experience despite staying near land.",
"bg|City Watch": "You served the community you grew up in as its first defense against crime, policing its people rather than facing outside enemies. You may have walked with Waterdeep's City Watch, Silverymoon's Silverwatch or Spellguard, or Neverwinter's Wintershield. Many towns have constables, sheriffs, and bailiffs who do this same work.",
"bg|Clan Crafter": "The Stout Folk are famed for their craftsmanship, and you trained in that old tradition under a dwarf master, enduring long hours and sour treatment to earn your skill. You are probably a dwarf, but shield dwarf clans accept promising apprentices of any race. Non-dwarves swear never to teach the craft onward.",
"bg|Cloistered Scholar": "As a child you were curious while your playmates were rowdy. You went to one of Faerûn's great centers of learning, such as Candlekeep, the Vault of the Sages, or Silverymoon's Map House, and learned that knowledge outweighs gold. Now you leave to seek new lore and share your expertise.",
"bg|Cobalt Scholar (Sage)": "The Cobalt Soul is an order of monastic scholars who preserve knowledge and history across Exandria, based mainly in Zadash and Rexxentrum. You likely studied there as a pupil and adventure to broaden your mind. With your DM's leave, you could instead be an expositor or an archivist.",
"bg|Cosmic Dawn Experiment": "The transmutation practices of the Bringers of the Cosmic Dawn have warped your body. You might have been caught in one of their experiments, or you might have joined and sought to improve yourself through magic. Either way, you now use your odd anatomy to your advantage.",
"bg|Courtier": "Once you held a position of some importance in a noble court or a bureaucracy, earned by talent rather than birth. You may have served in Silverymoon, Waterdeep, Baldur's Gate, Neverwinter, or Daggerford. Your old ties can help you and your companions, and the skills you honed there still serve you well.",
"bg|Covenant of the Grave Recruit": "Death has always fascinated you. You studied the morbid with members of the Covenant of the Grave, treating the macabre with scientific precision. Through this work you gained knowledge of medicine, necromancy, and history.",
"bg|Criminal": "You are an experienced lawbreaker with contacts across the criminal underworld. You know the murder, theft, and violence under civilization's surface better than most, and you have survived by ignoring the rules of society.",
"bg|Crucible Storm Chaser": "Your daredevil streak and hunger for thrills brought you to the Crucible Keepers, who study natural and magical disasters. You have handled molten glass and charged into the eye of a hurricane. You feel most alive when catastrophe is only steps away.",
"bg|Dead Magic Dweller": "Anauroch's dead magic zones are hostile to casters and magical monsters, and that is why you settled there. Maybe Red Wizards hunted you, or you angered a djinni in Calimshan. After months or years, you came out stronger and wiser, knowing desert medicine and wasteland survival.",
"bg|Dimir Operative": "You are a spy of House Dimir, dealing in secrets and lies, infiltrating guilds and stealing from journals and minds alike. Your missions sometimes only hide another strike or spread fear. You keep a false identity as a member of another guild, which supplies your gear and contacts.",
"bg|Discredited Neural Tuner": "You once worked at Mordenheim Kinetics doing questionable work in its darkest depths, until something angered the powers above and you were punished. Company security dragged you to a waste chamber for failed experiments, but you escaped. Now you live as a disgraced outcast, hunting the truth about your old employer.",
"bg|Dragon Cultist": "You are an initiate of the Cult of the Dragon, found or brought into a local cell. You show its values of duplicity, secrecy, and determination. In return for your oath, the cult gave you fellow worshipers and resources for studying arcana and the occult.",
"bg|Emerald Enclave Caretaker": "You serve the Emerald Enclave by caring for those who protect the world. Alone or with others, you learned to track game, forage herbs, and predict weather. You use these skills to keep civilization and wilderness in balance and to destroy unnatural creatures.",
"bg|Entertainer": "You thrive before an audience and know how to enchant, amuse, and inspire. Your verse can stir grief or joy, your music lifts spirits or mourns, your dance dazzles, and your humor stings. Whatever your methods, your art is your life.",
"bg|Fabricated Mind": "You are the product of monstrous experimental neurotechnology, a wholly new mind rather than a copy. You woke sentient in the halls of Mordenheim Kinetics, with no idea who made you. Your synthetic nature pushes you to seek an identity, allies of similar origin, or revenge on your makers.",
"bg|Faceless": "You believe your class, people, family, or sins keep you from ever being a hero, yet you press on. You left your old face behind and took on a new persona, dramatic or subtle, literal or not. This background focuses on the hero beneath the mask.",
"bg|Faction Agent": "Many Faerûn factions ignore borders and act wherever needed, using listeners, smugglers, sellswords, and haven keepers. You served one as its hands, head, and heart, openly or in secret, before adventuring. You may keep your membership, and adventuring may even raise your standing.",
"bg|Failed Merchant": "You came from a line of merchants or tried business yourself, but it all ended badly through bad luck, outside forces, or weak skill. You lost everything but gained experience and contacts. Your sense of commerce drew you toward Acquisitions Incorporated, where a franchise may await.",
"bg|Familiar Trainer": "A magical familiar bonded with you in youth and stayed beside you through adolescence. You cannot imagine life without your furry, feathery, scaly, or slimy friend. You learned to work as a team, and when adventure gets hard, your familiar reminds you of small joys.",
"bg|Far Traveler": "Most people on the Sword Coast never travel more than a few miles from where they were born, but you come from a remote land few have heard of. You came for your own reasons. Local ways may seem strange, and you may be a figure of curiosity wherever you go.",
"bg|Farmer": "You grew up close to the land. Years of tending animals and crops gave you patience and good health. You appreciate nature's generosity and respect its fury, and you carry that steady outlook into every trial you face.",
"bg|Feylost": "You vanished from your home plane as a child and grew up in the Feywild. Fey may have taken you kindly, you may have wandered in by chance, or evil Fey may have stolen you. You returned changed, haunted by the Feywild nearby, and you both fear and long for it.",
"bg|Fisher": "You spent your life on fishing boats or searching the shallows for the sea's bounty. You may come from a family of fisher folk, or perhaps the work was a way out of a bad situation. Either way, you grew to love the sea, the craft, and the endless horizon.",
"bg|Flaming Fist Mercenary": "The Flaming Fist is Baldur's Gate's main law enforcement, a mercenary guild led by the city's grand duke. You served in it and learned to head off trouble with a hard stare and to take deadly blows. Its members are among the toughest warriors on the Sword Coast, and you uphold that name.",
"bg|Folk Hero": "You come from humble roots, yet you are destined for more. Your home village already sees you as its champion. Your fate calls you to face the tyrants and monsters that menace ordinary people everywhere.",
"bg|Gambler": "You believe a little gold can turn into a lot, or into none. Good or bad, you cannot predict Lady Luck, who sometimes lifts you and sometimes drops you. Still, there is always someone willing to take a bet.",
}
